      "role": "F1 input fuse, 5A TIME-LAG per H1-a (supersedes A5's 4A). Chosen for the HIGHEST melting I2t (6.3 A^2s) of every 5A/1206 candidate found in a fresh live sweep - same methodology the architect used for the superseded 4A pick, since no 1206 part in this class publishes a true slow-blow curve; I2t is the best available proxy for time-lag/surge-withstand behaviour. Confirmed via the vendor's own datasheet (not just the LCSC attribute): the part is titled 'SMD Fuse (High Inrush Current Withstand)', UL248-1/248-14 tested, opens in under 5s at 250% rated current and 0.2-20ms at 1000% - genuinely time-lag behaviour, not fast-acting. TRADEOFF FLAGGED: this specific 5A variant is rated only 32V DC (the family derates voltage as current rating rises - the 1A-4A siblings are 63V, the 5A member is not). 32V exactly meets the task floor but gives less margin than the superseded 4A pick's 63V rating against the roughly 36V hot-plug ring modelled in blocks.md B3 (F1 sits upstream of D2's TVS clamp, so it sees the raw input transient unclamped). If more voltage margin is wanted, swap to the 63V alternate below at about 13x the unit cost."
